c35884aff8f50","quote":"•Inability to deliver energy across transmission facilities, including due to congestion, outages, extreme weather, physical or cyber events, delays in construction or upgrades, permitting or siting challenges, or interconnection constraints.","context":"•Risks associated with regulatory requirements that may extend the operation of our coal facilities beyond planned retirement dates and require additional investments.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":361,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"61991505de56319158eef0eddd5b9098ad569bf676c4afaa72849a1fd2ba5b18","quote":"Our utility operations, resource adequacy and system reliability are subject to long-term planning and project risks.","context":"•Inability to deliver energy across transmission facilities, including due to congestion, outages, extreme weather, physical or cyber events, delays in construction or upgrades, permitting or siting challenges, or interconnection constraints.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":362,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"c28549ffcfdb54198ffc618f18e471854b92d05e31fc571261af0239b2fde6bd","quote":"Our ability to reliably serve customer demand depends on the availability of sufficient generation and capacity resources. Changes in load growth, resource retirements, accreditation of resources, generation performance, extreme weather events, or delays in development or delivery of new resources, including the necessary transmission infrastructure, could affect resource adequacy and system reliability.","context":"Our utility operations, resource adequacy and system reliability are subject to long-term planning and project risks.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":363,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"aab6e4e09aa751f7176d01abfb9aedf936de0a60cb8503b514f8d3c43ea28750","quote":"Most utility investments are planned to be used for decades. Transmission and generation investments typically have long lead times and are planned well in advance of in-service dates and typically subject to long-term resource plans. These plans are based on numerous assumptions such as: sales growth, customer usage, commodity prices, economic activity, costs, regulatory mechanisms, customer behavior, available technology, equipment availability and public policy. Xcel Energy’s long-term resource plan is dependent on our ability to obtain required approvals (including regulatory approval in jurisdictions where Xcel Energy operates), develop necessary technical expertise, allocate and coordinate sufficient resources and adhere to budgets and timelines.","context":"Table of Contents","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":366,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"b7fa85540e83086675abfc050220f922e06868d8180b59722c5b84b493a6ce16","quote":"In addition, the long-term nature of both our planning processes and our asset lives are subject to risk. The utility sector is undergoing significant change (e.g., the addition of large loads, increases in energy efficiency, wider adoption of distributed generation and shifts away from fossil fuel generation to renewable generation). Customer adoption of these technologies and increased energy efficiency or other reductions in expected sales growth could result in excess transmission and generation resources, downward pressure on sales growth, and potentially stranded costs if we are not able to fully recover costs and investments. Additionally, increasing uncertainty surrounding federal policy to renewable deployment could negatively impact wind, solar and storage development.","context":"Most utility investments are planned to be used for decades. Transmission and generation investments typically have long lead times and are planned well in advance of in-service dates and typically subject to long-term resource plans. These plans are based on numerous assumptions such as: sales growth, customer usage, commodity prices, economic activity, costs, regulatory mechanisms, customer behavior, available technology, equipment availability and public policy. Xcel Energy’s long-term resource plan is dependent on our ability to obtain required approvals (including regulatory approval in jurisdictions where Xcel Energy operates), develop necessary technical expertise, allocate and coordinate sufficient resources and adhere to budgets and timelines.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":367,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"9cd658d6e95119ce7d7c6802e99648f2549109f439444e4f2e6ba18151cffee1","quote":"The magnitude and timing of resource additions and changes in customer demand may not coincide with evolving customer preference for generation resources and end-uses, which introduces further uncertainty into long-term planning. Efforts to electrify the transportation and building sectors to reduce GHG emissions may result in higher electric demand and lower natural gas demand over time. New data centers and crypto mining facilities could generate significant increase in demand. Higher electric demand may require us to adopt new technologies and make significant generation, transmission and distribution investments including advanced grid infrastructure, which increases exposure to overall grid instability and technology obsolescence. Enterprise level financial and customer billing technology systems may be unable to support the increasing customer complexity. Evolving stakeholder preference for lower emissions from generation sources and end-uses, like heating, may impact our resource mix and put pressure on our ability to recover capital investments in natural gas generation and delivery. Multiple states may not agree as to the appropriate resource mix, which may lead to costs to comply with one jurisdiction that are not recoverable across all jurisdictions served by the same assets.","context":"In addition, the long-term nature of both our planning processes and our asset lives are subject to risk. The utility sector is undergoing significant change (e.g., the addition of large loads, increases in energy efficiency, wider adoption of distributed generation and shifts away from fossil fuel generation to renewable generation). Customer adoption of these technologies and increased energy efficiency or other reductions in expected sales growth could result in excess transmission and generation resources, downward pressure on sales growth, and potentially stranded costs if we are not able to fully recover costs and investments. Additionally, increasing uncertainty surrounding federal policy to renewable deployment could negatively impact wind, solar and storage development.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":368,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"121f368a3365e9387a274fd1f1e14c090caf1f5961b8ad4fe1b73b20da47ea8c","quote":"We require inputs such as coal, natural gas, uranium and water. Lack of availability of these resources could jeopardize long-term operations of our facilities or make them uneconomic to operate.","context":"g customer preference for generation resources and end-uses, which introduces further uncertainty into long-term planning. Efforts to electrify the transportation and building sectors to reduce GHG emissions may result in higher electric demand and lower natural gas demand over time. New data centers and crypto mining facilities could generate significant increase in demand. Higher electric demand may require us to adopt new technologies and make significant generation, transmission and distribution investments including advanced grid infrastructure, which increases exposure to overall grid instability and technology obsolescence. Enterprise level financial and customer billing technology systems may be unable to support the increasing customer complexity. Evolving stakeholder preference for lower emissions from generation sources and end-uses, like heating, may impact our resource mix and put pressure on our ability to recover capital investments in natural gas generation and delivery. Multiple states may not agree as to the appropriate resource mix, which may lead to costs to comply with one jurisdiction that are not recoverable across all jurisdictions served by the same assets.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":369,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"11efc9b4e1463eae5d160bc1f7bf2c108ce2fd171e3baaa0d827ac1527f07496","quote":"Our utilities are highly dependent on suppliers to deliver components in accordance with short and long-term project schedules.","context":"We require inputs such as coal, natural gas, uranium and water. Lack of availability of these resources could jeopardize long-term operations of our facilities or make them uneconomic to operate.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":370,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"8dc332ccb66f5fc710b5703a2f06ecd12fa9e2b7d95f2ef2cb1f83ddae0b1d9d","quote":"Our products contain components that are globally sourced from suppliers. A shortage of key components in which an alternative supplier is not identified could significantly impact operations and project plans for Xcel Energy and our customers. Such impacts could include timing of projects and the potential for project cancellation. Failure to adhere to project budgets and timelines could adversely impact our results of operations, financial condition or cash flows.","context":"Our utilities are highly dependent on suppliers to deliver components in accordance with short and long-term project schedules.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":371,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"008f4a53f4936bf2f1d8e793742d0c2106ee415a355aff6788b16ac38be95dc2","quote":"We are subject to physical and financial risks associated with climate change and other weather, natural disaster and resource depletion impacts.","context":"Our products contain components that are globally sourced from suppliers. A shortage of key components in which an alternative supplier is not identified could significantly impact operations and project plans for Xcel Energy and our customers. Such impacts could include timing of projects and the potential for project cancellation. Failure to adhere to project budgets and timelines could adversely impact our results of operations, financial condition or cash flows.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":372,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"f0a4a90009fd64fe6094c38d221ef1bb89e4eab8f27553834e1f9f24ccd9c100","quote":"Climate change can create physical and financial risk. Physical risks include changes in weather conditions and extreme weather events. Our customers’ energy needs vary with weather. To the extent weather conditions are affected by climate change, customers’ energy use could increase or decrease. Increased energy use due to weather changes over the long-term may require us to invest in generating assets, transmission and infrastructure. Decreased energy use due to weather changes may result in decreased revenues.","context":"We are subject to physical and financial risks associated with climate change and other weather, natural disaster and resource depletion impacts.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":373,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"b773104640f04785dced3067cc02ee466040318c25f09a21aad37ce545c413e5","quote":"Severe weather impacts our service territories, primarily when thunderstorms, flooding, tornadoes, wildfires, snow, ice storms or extreme temperatures (high heating/cooling days) occur. Extreme weather conditions in general require system backup and can contribute to increased system stress, including service interruptions. Extreme weather conditions creating high energy demand may raise electricity prices, increasing the cost of energy we provide to our customers.","context":"Climate change can create physical and financial risk. Physical risks include changes in weather conditions and extreme weather events. Our customers’ energy needs vary with weather. To the extent weather conditions are affected by climate change, customers’ energy use could increase or decrease. Increased energy use due to weather changes over the long-term may require us to invest in generating assets, transmission and infrastructure. Decreased energy use due to weather changes may result in decreased revenues.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":374,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"5da9cfd9b7848ab790ac81fd4d9b069adbcf1a8d236cd83e406032b83742202a","quote":"To the extent the frequency of extreme weather events increases, this could increase our cost of providing service and result in more frequent service interruptions. Periods of extreme temperatures could also impact our ability to meet demand.","context":"Severe weather impacts our service territories, primarily when thunderstorms, flooding, tornadoes, wildfires, snow, ice storms or extreme temperatures (high heating/cooling days) occur. Extreme weather conditions in general require system backup and can contribute to increased system stress, including service interruptions. Extreme weather conditions creating high energy demand may raise electricity prices, increasing the cost of energy we provide to our customers.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":375,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"b834ede985ab416a5d6cfee84f9afcb663f3795d933dec32f648489c868013e7","quote":"Drought or water depletion could adversely impact our ability to provide electricity to customers, cause early retirement of power plants that require water or increase the cost for energy.","context":"To the extent the frequency of extreme weather events increases, this could increase our cost of providing service and result in more frequent service interruptions. Periods of extreme temperatures could also impact our ability to meet demand.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":376,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"11c5392f0d9348fcb66722d2e8f66ef61e975c116d1939204c9efd95dae693cc","quote":"Adverse events may result in increased insurance costs and/or decreased insurance availability. We may not recover all costs related to mitigating these physical and financial risks.","context":"Drought or water depletion could adversely impact our ability to provide electricity to customers, cause early retirement of power plants that require water or increase the cost for energy.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":377,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"93c678a5b54feca36f9d43acadbe82fafe3055698af718367571bc631da336ef","quote":"Our utilities have significant risks associated with wildfires.","context":"Adverse events may result in increased insurance costs and/or decreased insurance availability. We may not recover all costs related to mitigating these physical and financial risks.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":378,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"614527201eac1b6d34ae5e77b3d4e80b1ef048033b3c82e4377709384fcbb23f","quote":"In recent years, wildfires have impacted the utility industry. More frequent and severe drought conditions, extreme swings in amount and timing of precipitation, changes in availability of vegetation, unseasonably warm temperatures, very low humidity, stronger winds and other environmental factors have increased both the frequency and duration of fire weather conditions and the potential impact of an event. The expansion of the wildland urban interface increases the wildfire risk to surrounding communities and Xcel Energy's electric and natural gas infrastructure. Also, wildfires could jeopardize Xcel Energy’s electric and gas infrastructure and third-party property and result in temporary power outages or shortages in our service territories. Our current wildfire mitigation initiatives may not be effective in preventing or reducing ignitions and wildfire-related losses.","context":"Our utilities have significant risks associated with wildfires.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":379,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"1b4e22288b55b94291ebedc4841ad45671bae47f4eee92d54ae0c7f9993ff8f2","quote":"Other potential risks associated with wildfires and other climate events include the inability to secure sufficient insurance coverage, increased costs of insurance, or ability for insurers to meet their obligations, regulatory recovery risk, and the potential for a credit downgrade and subsequent additional costs to access capital markets.","context":"In recent years, wildfires have impacted the utility industry. More frequent and severe drought conditions, extreme swings in amount and timing of precipitation, changes in availability of vegetation, unseasonably warm temperatures, very low humidity, stronger winds and other environmental factors have increased both the frequency and duration of fire weather conditions and the potential impact of an event. The expansion of the wildland urban interface increases the wildfire risk to surrounding communities and Xcel Energy's electric and natural gas infrastructure. Also, wildfires could jeopardize Xcel Energy’s electric and gas infrastructure and third-party property and result in temporary power outages or shortages in our service territories. Our current wildfire mitigation initiatives may not be effective in preventing or reducing ignitions and wildfire-related losses.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":380,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"532104be44fa8a1a7b65127b9aef2be259e36d30cb847b61ae0902e69af5deb7","quote":"While we carry liability insurance, given an extreme event, damage amounts could exceed our coverage (as experienced with the Marshall Wildfire settlement in 2025) and negatively impact our results of operations, financial condition or cash flows.","context":"Other potential risks associated with wildfires and other climate events include the inability to secure sufficient insurance coverage, increased costs of insurance, or ability for insurers to meet their obligations, regulatory recovery risk, and the potential for a credit downgrade and subsequent additional costs to access capital markets.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":381,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"940c80dc4ce404e31935d8d346e08cc453e2e25a1fd56708ca6c74836752c36a","quote":"We are subject to commodity risks and other risks associated with energy markets and energy production.","context":"Table of Contents","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":384,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"181ebffead19b28a4f0a4a1f965dd1b304b449814f66e0db9a9c620015782b01","quote":"A significant increase in fuel costs could cause a decline in customer demand, adverse regulatory outcomes and an increase in bad debt expense which may have a material impact on our results of operations. Despite existing fuel cost recovery mechanisms in most of our states, higher fuel costs could significantly impact our results of operations if costs are not recovered. Delays in the timing of the collection of fuel cost recoveries could impact our cash flows and liquidity.","context":"We are subject to commodity risks and other risks associated with energy markets and energy production.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":385,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"cb508f169578341fe5824f2aafcf652dc9687bf671852180e975cf46111b1e7a","quote":"A significant disruption in supply could cause us to seek alternatives at potentially higher costs. Additionally, supply shortages may not be fully resolved, which negatively impacts our ability to provide services to our customers. Failure to provide service due to disruptions may also result in fines, penalties or cost disallowances through the regulatory process.","context":"A significant increase in fuel costs could cause a decline in customer demand, adverse regulatory outcomes and an increase in bad debt expense which may have a material impact on our results of operations. Despite existing fuel cost recovery mechanisms in most of our states, higher fuel costs could significantly impact our results of operations if costs are not recovered. Delays in the timing of the collection of fuel cost recoveries could impact our cash flows and liquidity.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":386,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"90a6bb3fb0c2fe5ff7d07c3184feb6b54a16057d8ea88208b27cb383044a68dd","quote":"We also engage in wholesale sales and purchases of electric capacity, energy and energy-related products as well as natural gas. In many markets, emission allowances and/or RECs are also needed to comply with various statutes and commission rulings. As a result, we are subject to market supply and commodity price risk.","context":"A significant disruption in supply could cause us to seek alternatives at potentially higher costs. Additionally, supply shortages may not be fully resolved, which negatively impacts our ability to provide services to our customers. Failure to provide service due to disruptions may also result in fines, penalties or cost disallowances through the regulatory process.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":387,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"34bdb513486c556c4cd4215dd9f6f5b6e6b0a28e3b2286343629275de9cd7b47","quote":"Commodity price changes can affect the value of our commodity trading derivatives. We mark certain derivatives to estimated fair market value on a daily basis. Settlements can vary significantly from estimated fair values recorded and significant changes from the assumptions underlying our fair value estimates could cause earnings variability. The management of risks associated with hedging and trading is based, in part, on programs and procedures which utilize historical prices and trends.","context":"We also engage in wholesale sales and purchases of electric capacity, energy and energy-related products as well as natural gas. In many markets, emission allowances and/or RECs are also needed to comply with various statutes and commission rulings. As a result, we are subject to market supply and commodity price risk.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":388,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"d6829990527e99706401db4d7ac9114f6dd082a19318e00c0d820530f17d7463","quote":"Public perception often does not distinguish between pass through commodity costs and base rates. High commodity prices that are passed through to customer bills could impact our ability to recover costs for other improvements and operations.","context":"Commodity price changes can affect the value of our commodity trading derivatives. We mark certain derivatives to estimated fair market value on a daily basis. Settlements can vary significantly from estimated fair values recorded and significant changes from the assumptions underlying our fair value estimates could cause earnings variability. The management of risks associated with hedging and trading is based, in part, on programs and procedures which utilize historical prices and trends.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":389,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558.bin","sha256":"15386a65d56ac573e94975738f070b69b8abc971ec1051e37593c49e44b14558"}},{"id":"ef76fed7b5b07e9c7e39747b12a04f0fb041fc23f993eb00f41df95dd48c149b","quote":"Additionally, due to the uncertainty involved in price movements and potential deviation from historical pricing, our risk management programs may not be effective to protect against significant adverse market fluctuations and our results of operations, financial condition or cash flows could be materially impacted.","context":"Public perception often does not distinguish between pass through commodity costs and base rates. High commodity prices that are passed through to customer bills could impact our ability to recover costs for other improvements and operations.","section":"The occurrence of these events, if not fully covered by insurance, could have a material effect on our financial condition, results of operations and cash flows as well as potential reputational impact.","block":390,"table":null,"attribution":"management","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000072903-26-000009","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-25","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000072903","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/72903/000007290326000009/xel-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/15386